[2026-03-14T23:37:08.818Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H5pHSvkb7AnuAzd7jVCdtg","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a tool for recording application sessions to gather performance data and debug issues. Here's what you need to know:\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nSet up your API key from https://app.replay.io settings and store it in the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n- **`replayio record [url]`** - Launch Replay Browser and begin recording a session\n- **`replayio upload [ids...]`** - Upload recordings to the cloud\n- **`replayio list`** - View all local recordings\n- **`replayio remove [ids...]`** - Delete recordings\n- **`replayio login/logout`** - Manage authentication\n- **`replayio info`** - Check installed dependencies and versions\n\n## Quick Workflow\n\nRecord a session, then upload it:\n```sh\nreplayio record https://myapp.com\nreplayio upload <recording-id>\n```\n\n## MCP Integration\n\nThe Replay MCP Server enables Claude to analyze recorded sessions. Install it with the provided configuration using your `REPLAY_API_KEY`.\n\nFor complete details on options and arguments, run `replayio -h` or consult the command reference above."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a0e50a1-d387-4a98-a992-f6a73a5e58c8","uuid":"622b7678-e128-420f-b0c3-16f261f0c80a","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Reference\n\n**Replay CLI** is a tool for recording application sessions to gather performance data and debug issues. Here's what you need to know:\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\n## Authentication\n\nSet up your API key from https://app.replay.io settings and store it in the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\n- **`replayio record [url]`** - Launch Replay Browser and begin recording a session\n- **`replayio upload [ids...]`** - Upload recordings to the cloud\n- **`replayio list`** - View all local recordings\n- **`replayio remove [ids...]`** - Delete recordings\n- **`replayio login/logout`** - Manage authentication\n- **`replayio info`** - Check installed dependencies and versions\n\n## Quick Workflow\n\nRecord a session, then upload it:\n```sh\nreplayio record https://myapp.com\nreplayio upload <recording-id>\n```\n\n## MCP Integration\n\nThe Replay MCP Server enables Claude to analyze recorded sessions. Install it with the provided configuration using your `REPLAY_API_KEY`.\n\nFor complete details on options and arguments, run `replayio -h` or consult the command reference above.","durationMs":3229,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-14T23:37:09.192Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NDDoK8c8rZgPqbTC4s4RE8","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Documentation\n\nReplay MCP is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution data. According to the documentation, \"Replay MCP is used to inspect the contents of https://replay.io recordings.\"\n\n## Key Workflow\n\nThe recommended investigation approach involves four steps:\n1. Frame your task as a question\n2. Use exploratory tools to identify relevant events\n3. Use explanatory tools to understand causation\n4. Form and support a hypothesis with gathered data\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** discover what happened and when, including error detection, timeline analysis, and source code examination.\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events occurred through dependency tracking and state inspection.\n\n## Core Concepts\n\nThe documentation defines two essential terms: a \"Point\" represents a specific execution moment when a JS statement runs or significant event occurs, while an \"Element\" refers to a DOM element reference independent of any particular point.\n\nThe guidance emphasizes avoiding premature conclusions: \"The most important thing to remember when using Replay MCP to investigate a recording is to not jump to conclusions.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1a0e50a1-d387-4a98-a992-f6a73a5e58c8","uuid":"175a630b-76f6-4173-8ba3-9e747559d8ca","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Documentation\n\nReplay MCP is a tool for inspecting Replay.io recordings, which capture complete browser execution data. According to the documentation, \"Replay MCP is used to inspect the contents of https://replay.io recordings.\"\n\n## Key Workflow\n\nThe recommended investigation approach involves four steps:\n1. Frame your task as a question\n2. Use exploratory tools to identify relevant events\n3. Use explanatory tools to understand causation\n4. Form and support a hypothesis with gathered data\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** discover what happened and when, including error detection, timeline analysis, and source code examination.\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events occurred through dependency tracking and state inspection.\n\n## Core Concepts\n\nThe documentation defines two essential terms: a \"Point\" represents a specific execution moment when a JS statement runs or significant event occurs, while an \"Element\" refers to a DOM element reference independent of any particular point.\n\nThe guidance emphasizes avoiding premature conclusions: \"The most important thing to remember when using Replay MCP to investigate a recording is to not jump to conclusions.\"","durationMs":3055,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-mcp/SKILL.md"}}
