before e6666666 gets cancelled in test 2, and test 6 will work since the booking will already be in the cancelled state it expects.\n\nWait, I'm seeing the real problem now: tests 2, 6, and 7 all cancel the same booking (e6666666), but only the first cancellation will succeed in a shared database. The other two will fail because the booking is already cancelled. This is a fundamental data contamination issue—each test assumes a fresh initial state, but they're all operating on the same booking.\n\nThe best solution is to either use different booking IDs for each cancellation test, or restructure the tests so only one actually performs the cancellation and the others verify different aspects without modifying the same data. I could also add a `beforeEach` hook to reset the booking state, but that requires the app to support restoration or the database to reset between tests. tests 2, 6, 7).\n\nHmm, maybe the fix is different. Let me look at the seed data to see if there are other confirmed bookings we can use for tests 4 and others.\n\nActually, let me re-read the failing tests more carefully:\n\nTest 4 (overlay click): Opens dialog for e6666666, clicks overlay to close - NON-DESTRUCTIVE. It should work before test 2 runs.\n\nTest 6 (moves from upcoming to cancelled): Cancels e6666666 - DESTRUCTIVE. Same as test 2 essentially.\n\nTest 7: Dismisses e3333333, then cancels e6666666 - DESTRUCTIVE.\n\nSo the fix should be:\n- Move tests 4, 5, 7 BEFORE test 2\n- And have tests 2 and 6 as the last two\n\nBut wait, both tests 2 and 6 are cancelling the same booking e6666666, so only one can actually succeed. They're essentially testing the same cancellation flow, except test 6 also verifies the red badge appears in the Cancelled tab. Once that booking is cancelled, it won't be available for the second test to run against. I need to either give them different bookings to work with or rethink the approach entirely. Let me check what other confirmed bookings exist in the seed d